				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">Journal of an Adventurer, found clutched in a skeleton's hand. 
<br />
Entry Date: October 1, 2004
<br />

<br />
Journal - I have gained access to new lands today, via my contacts in the Discord Priesthood. My wonderful friend in Qeynos (you remember him, we used to take a spot of ale on the docks a few years back?), well, he was gracious enough to allow me to be one of the first to journey across space and time into a new land recently discovered through their chaos magic. I simply had to instruct him that I "wished to go" and he said "Prepare yourself to enter a land of Discord!!" or some such dramatic nonsense, anyway, I took a swallow of gin from my flask and said "Yar, Johnny just send me over you pompous fool" which was rather out of character for me but then I'd been drinking since early that morning..
<br />

<br />
Ahem...well, back to the story. So here I go being whisked away into a place called "Dranik's Scar". (First off, let me tell you that the journey was rather long, and my seat was very uncomfortable, and the tray table kept getting stuck and I bumped my knee on it quite a few times. I'd advise not to travel this way but the Priests have a monopoly at the moment, those bastards) I arrived in the deep murk of night. I was instructed to cast a cloak of invisibility on myself, as the locals were a tad unfriendly, and with a cheery wave to my friends the Priests, I set off across the rolling hills.
<br />

<br />
Rolling hills my ass!!! I promptly fell off the face of a rather high cliff, which was hidden in the murk (to be able to move I've had to turn my visibility down to 0, which makes for treacherous footing) and tumbled nearly to my death at the bottom. Thankfully I'm quite tough from my years of adventuring, and by sitting still for three hours I was able to regen my health to an acceptable level. Off I went, headed now towards the dark form of a castle that I could just barely make out. 
<br />

<br />
At this point I became aware of a flood of other adventurers and travelers entering the castle. (One of the first eh Johnny you cheatin bastard!) I called out a "hullo" to the passerbys, thinking perhaps to tag along with one of their groups and get some first impressions. They ran by as if I were not even there, their faces drawn taunt with expressions of deep thought and earnest anxiety. I wondered what could cause such rampant distraction, and followed them inside. Apparently I had hit one of those "zone lines" that are the magical boundaries between areas in our world, as the next five minutes I spent gazing into nothingness while the words "Loading, Please Wait" flashed across my field of vision. 
<br />

<br />
The next leg of my journey was along a broad stone pathway known as the "Noble's Causeway". It was in a state of disrepair, and I was forced to  climb over piles of rocks to make my way. At every turn was stationed a strange creature of the area, natives I supposed, but their stern and unfriendly expressions made me wary to approach. I thanked Tunare for my cloak of invisibility and struck on. By following the map my friend the Priest had given me, I could see that I was nearly to the next "zoneline" when abruptly my cloak fell!!! I was visible, and suddenly the gaze of the unfriendly locals was focused on me!! What's more, I could clearly tell that they were scowling, and ready to attack! As an adventurer, I know exactly how to handle myself in these situations.
<br />

<br />
I ran screaming "TRAIN" towards the zone line. 
<br />

<br />
I hit the thing just as the nearest creature reached out a claw to rend me from head to toe. Thankfully I passed into the barrier and it was left behind. I calmed myself while waiting to cross into the next area, which took quite some time, so I was able to have a few swallows of my gin, only to aid in the calming of my nerves, of course. 
<br />

<br />
Entering the next area, which on my map, was marked "Wall of Slaughter" (doesnt that sound cheery?), and my final destination, I quickly threw on a  new cloak of invisibility and got my bearings. I was in a dark hall, plain stone, surrounded by hundreds of other adventurers. The air was full of cries: "65 Mage LFG!" "66 Warr LFG" "65 Wizard LFG" "67 Rogue LFG" and so on and so forth. It was deafening, and the crowd of people made it impossible to move. I readjusted my viewer settings to tone down color, spell effects, far sight and was soon successfully able to move but only able to see 1 foot infront of me. I bumped into quite a few people and walls on my way out of the tunnel. "Pardon, so sorry, pardon me, sorry.."
<br />

<br />
Breaking free of the tunnel, I was confronted with an astounding view. Brown fog, as far as the eye could see!! With the viewer settings this low, the environment was lost on me. I headed out, joining the others in the righteous cry of the adventurer, LFG!! 
<br />

<br />
Two hours later I was quite drunk on a hill, where four or five others had taken up camp while desperately searching for companions.  We were passing around the gin flask and having a wonderful time, really, when suddenly a terrible scream tore through the air. "Shadowhunter!!!!" A mad halfling screached, running by as fast as his little hairy feet could take him.
<br />

<br />
"I say, what is this creature shouting about?" I asked. "He's disturbed the peace."
<br />

<br />
"Aye mate ye better get up yer cloak, for that Shadowhunter is on the prowl."
<br />

<br />
"I'm not yer mate."
<br />

<br />
"I know you're not my mate, but I just say mate a lot."
<br />

<br />
"Why?"
<br />

<br />
"Because I'm a dwarf."
<br />

<br />
"Oh. That must be hard."
<br />

<br />
I shrugged, wondering why the gentleman looked so put out, and rummaged in my bags for a potion. Curses, I was out!! I turned back to my offended companion.
<br />

<br />
"I say fellow, have you an extra potion?"
<br />

<br />
"I'm not your fellow." 
<br />

<br />
"C'mon, really, do you?"
<br />

<br />
"I don't got nothin fer the likes of you." And with that he stamped off, but he was drinking a potion and I saw another in his bags! Stingy bastard!! Well, it looked like I had one choice only. Head up to the very highest point on the hill and hope for the best. In my drunken state I stumbled as best I could up the hill and did my best to look like a rock, so this Shadowhunter thing wouldn't notice me.  I was complimenting myself on my rock impression when the world began to shake. I looked up with horror as a huge spikey dog-like thing ran by, he was blue and spotty and his tongue was hanging from his fanged mouth. It was roaring and running fast, his claws gouging deep furrows into the earth. But he was running past me! I was saved!
<br />

<br />
Just when I was standing up and brushing off the dirt, I was hit by a huge shock wave and fell in a death-like position to the ground once more. I was completely paralyzed, and had taken some damage from the blast. What in the seven planes was this? 
<br />

<br />
After a bit, I was able to move again. I headed down the hill to try and find someone to ask about the strange phenomenon. All I found was corpses of fallen adventurers. I headed back down the tunnel that lead to Noble's Causeway and greeted the group of naked folk huddled there. They eyed me peevishly. 
<br />

<br />
"Well now fellows this isn't the place to have a streaker's orgy." I grinned. A rather nice-looking high elf female glared at me.
<br />

<br />
"Shadowhunter wiped our group." She said. I frowned. So, this creature was formidable. And I assumed the large shock wave was it's work. Strong magic, and deadly. It would make further adventuring highly risky. Especially alone. 
<br />

<br />
"Well, say, could I join you in your adventuring?" I asked hopefully. They glanced at each other, and shrugged. "Fine, we need another."
<br />

<br />
I was invited into their group. We stood there for some time, and I shared my gin to warm them up while they waited for resurrection. It took about two hours to get the process completed, as the cleric they'd employed for the job was a bit of a jumpy fellow and kept gating back to his safe spot and having to run back. At last though, they were dressed and ready to kill some creatures.
<br />

<br />
Another few minutes was used in the decision of where to set up camp, with much disagreement of the companions. One wanted an easy camp, another one with "nameds". At last we decided to head for the wall at the center of the zone, where we set up our camp, after locating three lost members who wandered off in the wrong direction. This took about an hour.
<br />

<br />
All together at last, and at our camp, we sent the young monk off to find a creature and bring it back for slaughtering. Perhaps this sounds a bit uncouth but I assure you it's all we have to do really and by killing the creatures we gain valuable memories that advance our skills, and also sometimes items that can be sold for coin. 
<br />

<br />
Our hapless monk returned battered but with a creature in tow. The tank set to, drawing the creature's attention whilst the rest of us attacked it with magic and weapons. It died without mishap, and the monk set off again.
<br />

<br />
I was just beginning to feel the rythm of this place when our ranger, a shady half elf, spied a creature wandering near our camp.
<br />

<br />
"A named!!" he cried, and as if all intelligence had left him, ran off to capture it's attention and bring it back. At the same time, the monk returned with two creatures in tow. Almost too fast to know what was happened, the three creatures descended apon our camp. Confused, the tank attempted to grab the attention of the "named", but was distracted by the second creature savaging our cleric. The third creature was making mincemeat out of the monk. I swallowed a lump in my throat and hastily penned these last few lines. 
<br />

<br />
I see my death approaching, the cleric is done for, and the tank has tried to run the creatures off but it was futile, he is dead, and the creatures know my scent and are almost apon me. I must leave these words for those who come after me, beware these walls!<div style="vertical-align: bottom"><br /><br /><br />_________________<br /><a href="http://www.magelo.com/eq_view_profile.html?num=956867" target="_blank" class="postlink">~Sriani HeartofWolf~</a>
<br />
66 Lady of War
<br />
<span style="font-weight: bold">Wolves of Zek Recruit Council</span></div></span><span class="gensmall"></span></td>
